### What is the Stream API?

The Stream API (java.util.stream) was introduced in Java 8 for declarative data processing. Instead of writing imperative for-loops with explicit accumulators, you describe what to compute and the framework handles execution.

### What are the three components?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class StreamComponentsDemo {
    record Transaction(String accountId, BigDecimal amount, boolean settled) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Transaction> transactions = List.of(
            new Transaction("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("45000"), true),
            new Transaction("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("12000"), false),
            new Transaction("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("78000"), true),
            new Transaction("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("250000"), true)
        );

        BigDecimal total = transactions.stream()              // SOURCE
            .filter(Transaction::settled)                      // INTERMEDIATE (lazy)
            .map(Transaction::amount)                          // INTERMEDIATE (lazy)
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);         // TERMINAL (triggers all)

        System.out.println("Settled total: Rs." + total);
    }
}
```

```text
Settled total: Rs.373000
```

### How is a Stream different from a Collection?

| Aspect | Collection | Stream |
|--------|-----------|--------|
| Storage | Stores elements in memory | Stores nothing: processes on the fly |
| Evaluation | Eager: all elements exist upfront | Lazy: computed only when terminal runs |
| Reuse | Reusable: iterate many times | Single-use: consumed after terminal |
| Mutability | Can add, remove, modify | Cannot modify source |
| How you work | Imperative: for-loop, if, accumulator | Declarative: filter, map, collect |
| Parallelism | Manual thread management | Built-in: parallelStream() |

### What are the stream sources?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StreamSources {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // From Collection:
        List.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002").stream();

        // From array:
        Stream.of("RELIANCE", "TCS", "HDFC");
        Arrays.stream(new int[]{100, 200, 300});

        // From generator (infinite!):
        Stream.generate(() -> BigDecimal.valueOf(Math.random()));
        Stream.iterate(BigDecimal.ONE, amt -> amt.multiply(BigDecimal.TWO));

        // Bounded iterate (Java 9):
        Stream.iterate(1, n -> n <= 100, n -> n + 1);

        // Empty:
        Stream.empty();
    }
}
```

### What are the intermediate operations?

All intermediate operations are lazy and return a new Stream.

- `filter(Predicate)` : keep matching elements
- `map(Function)` : transform each element
- `flatMap(Function)` : transform and flatten (one-to-many)
- `sorted()` / `sorted(Comparator)` : sort (stateful, buffers all)
- `distinct()` : remove duplicates (stateful, uses HashSet)
- `limit(n)` : take first n (short-circuit)
- `skip(n)` : drop first n
- `peek(Consumer)` : debug observation (do not use for business logic)

### What are the terminal operations?

Terminal operations trigger execution and consume the stream.

- `collect(Collector)` : accumulate into collection, string, or map
- `toList()` : collect to unmodifiable list (Java 16)
- `forEach(Consumer)` : execute action on each
- `reduce(identity, BinaryOperator)` : fold into single value
- `count()` : count elements
- `findFirst()` / `findAny()` : return Optional (short-circuit)
- `anyMatch` / `allMatch` / `noneMatch` : boolean (short-circuit)
- `min(Comparator)` / `max(Comparator)` : return Optional

### What happens if you reuse a stream?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class StreamReuseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stream<String> stream = Stream.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002", "CUR3001");
        long count = stream.count(); // OK: terminal #1
        System.out.println("Count: " + count);

        try {
            stream.toList(); // THROWS IllegalStateException!
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Count: 3
Error: stream has already been operated upon or closed
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.List;

public class StreamMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002");

        // WRONG: no terminal operation, pipeline does nothing
        accounts.stream()
            .filter(a -> a.startsWith("SAV"))
            .peek(System.out::println); // Nothing printed! No terminal!

        // CORRECT: add a terminal operation
        accounts.stream()
            .filter(a -> a.startsWith("SAV"))
            .forEach(System.out::println); // Prints both accounts
    }
}
```

```text
SAV2001
SAV2002
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Streams are designed to complement Collections, not replace them. Collections store data (the data layer). Streams process data (the computation layer). The typical pattern is: `collection.stream()` followed by pipeline operations followed by `collect()` back to a Collection. Streams are particularly powerful for read-only analytical queries, aggregation, grouping, and parallel processing of large datasets.
